      "text": "To determine the number of the first day that ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria, we start by understanding the pattern of growth in the colony. The colony starts with 3 bacteria and doubles every day. We can express the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) as \\( 3 \\times 2^n \\).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( 3 \\times 2^n > 100 \\).\n\nFirst, let's solve the inequality for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^n > 100 \\]\n\\[ 2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\]\n\\[ 2^n > 33.33 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies this inequality. We can do this by calculating powers of 2:\n\n\\[ 2^5 = 32 \\]\n\\[ 2^6 = 64 \\]\n\nSince \\( 2^5 = 32 \\) is less than 33.33 and \\( 2^6 = 64 \\) is greater than 33.33, the smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies the inequality is 6.\n\nTo verify, we can calculate the number of bacteria at the end of day 6:\n\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^6 = 3 \\times 64 = 192 \\]\n\nSince 192 is indeed greater than 100, the first day that ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is day 6.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
