      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of days it takes for the colony of bacteria to exceed 100, we start by understanding the pattern of growth. The colony starts with 3 bacteria and doubles each day. We can represent the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) as \\( 3 \\times 2^n \\).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( 3 \\times 2^n > 100 \\).\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality:\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^n > 100. \\]\nDividing both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[ 2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\approx 33.33. \\]\nSince \\( n \\) must be an integer, we need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( 2^n > 33.33 \\).\n\nWe can calculate the values of \\( 2^n \\) for successive integers of \\( n \\):\n\\[ 2^2 = 4, \\]\n\\[ 2^3 = 8, \\]\n\\[ 2^4 = 16, \\]\n\\[ 2^5 = 32, \\]\n\\[ 2^6 = 64, \\]\n\\[ 2^7 = 128. \\]\n\nWe see that \\( 2^6 = 64 \\) is less than 33.33, and \\( 2^7 = 128 \\) is greater than 33.33. Therefore, the smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies the inequality is \\( n = 7 \\).\n\nTo confirm, we calculate the number of bacteria at the end of day 7:\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^7 = 3 \\times 128 = 384. \\]\nSince \\( 384 > 100 \\), the colony has more than 100 bacteria at the end of day 7.\n\nThus, the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
